{%- doc -%}
Styling shared by every "zoomable media" item rendered via snippets/media-zoom-slide.liquid: the
video sound/play-pause/product controls (position, hover, touch relocation next to a progress bar)
and the zoom-to-lightbox expand/collapse look. This is the Carousel block's own <style> block
(sections/media-blocks.liquid) generalized so it can be shared with any other caller that renders
zoomable media items (eg. FAQ's "Text + image/video" block).

Must be rendered inside a <style> tag by the caller, once per instance (wrapper_id/lightbox_id pair).

@param {string} wrapper_id - id of the ancestor element wrapping every media item and the lightbox (same id passed to snippets/media-zoom-script.liquid). Also used to scope the lightbox-expanded positioning rules - video-media/img stay descendants of this wrapper in the DOM even once they visually escape to position: fixed, so scoping to it (rather than needing the enclosing section's id) is enough and avoids an extra id having to be threaded through every caller.
@param {string} lightbox_id - id of the media-lightbox element
@param {string} [corner_radius] - CSS value matching the media item's own border-radius (eg. "var(--media-blocks-corner-radius)" or "12px"), reused so the expanded lightbox media keeps the same rounded corners
@param {string} [controls_position] - 'top' (default) or 'progress_bar'
@param {string} [desktop_controls_position] - 'top_right' (default) or 'center', only used when controls_position is 'progress_bar'
@param {string} [controls_slot_id] - id of the element controls are relocated into on touch devices, required when controls_position is 'progress_bar'
@param {boolean} [has_multiple_slides] - whether there is an actual progress bar to reorder/hide the product link next to (carousel-only)
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign resolved_corner_radius = corner_radius | default: '0px'
  assign resolved_controls_position = controls_position | default: 'top'
  assign resolved_desktop_controls_position = desktop_controls_position | default: 'top_right'
-%}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > img,
#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > video {
  opacity: 1 !important;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > img {
  z-index: 1;
  transition: opacity .3s ease;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > img.carousel-video-poster-hidden {
  opacity: 0 !important;
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* If autoplay is blocked by the browser, the shared video-media component falls back to the native
   play button (sets [suspended] + controls) so playback can still be started manually. Our poster
   image would otherwise sit on top of it and hide/block taps. */
#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media[suspended] > img {
  opacity: 0 !important;
  pointer-events: none;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-tap-to-play {
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* Root cause of controls inheriting Color Scheme colors: .circle-button (theme-wide, assets/theme.css)
   paints a scheme-derived tint via its own ::before pseudo-element on top of whatever background we
   set, and shifts icon `color` on hover via a separate scheme-derived color-mix(). Both are killed
   below for our buttons specifically, the same way the theme already opts individual variants out via
   ".circle-button--outline:before { content: none; }". From here on, background/border/icon color
   come exclusively from the --control-button-* variables (Global > Controls). */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle:before,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle:before,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link:before {
  content: none;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link {
  transition: opacity 0.2s ease, background-color 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease, color 0.2s ease;
}

/* Base position (top-right, matches "top" layout exactly). Declared before the device/layout rules
   below on purpose: the "center" override further down has the same specificity (#id .class), so it
   only wins because it comes later in the cascade. */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
  width: auto;
  height: auto;
  position: absolute;
  inset-block-start: 1rem;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
  z-index: 1;
}

/* Always visible, no hover/tap-to-reveal, no auto-hide: nothing autoplays, so there's no playing
   video to hide these behind - the visitor needs to see the play button right away to know how to
   start it. */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link {
  opacity: 1;
  pointer-events: auto;
}

{%- if resolved_controls_position == 'progress_bar' -%}
  {%- if resolved_desktop_controls_position == 'center' -%}
    @media screen and (pointer: fine) {
      #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
        inset-block-start: 50%;
        inset-inline-end: auto;
        inset-inline-start: 50%;
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
      }
    }
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
  Desktop never moves controls into the slot, so the slot is always empty here - hide it outright so
  it stops taking up gap/space and the progress bar's own flex-grow fills 100% of the row.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (pointer: fine) {
    #{{ controls_slot_id }} {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  {%- comment -%}
  Mobile is the only device that relocates the controls group into the slot, so it is the only one
  that needs to hide it at its original, overlaid-on-video position and only show it once it is an
  actual descendant of the slot.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (pointer: coarse) {
    #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
      display: none;
    }

    #{{ controls_slot_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
      display: flex;
      position: static;
      inset: auto;
    }
  }
{%- endif -%}

@media screen and (pointer: fine) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle:not([disabled]):hover,
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle:not([disabled]):hover,
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link:not([disabled]):hover {
    background: var(--control-button-hover-background-color, var(--control-button-background-color, #333333));
    color: var(--control-button-icon-color, #ffffff);
  }
}

/* Text mode reuses the theme's body text styling as-is (no independent typography). */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link__text {
  font: var(--text-font-style) var(--text-font-weight) var(--text-base) / var(--text-line-height) var(--text-font-family);
  text-transform: var(--text-transform);
  letter-spacing: var(--text-letter-spacing);
  white-space: nowrap;
}

{%- if resolved_controls_position == 'progress_bar' and has_multiple_slides -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The product link stays hidden in this layout on both devices. Controls sit on the left, progress
    bar fills the rest on the right, on mobile - same two flex children as always, just reordered
    visually via the standard flexbox `order` property, not duplicated markup.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link {
    display: none;
  }

  #{{ wrapper_id }} .progress-bar.grow {
    order: 2;
  }

  #{{ controls_slot_id }} {
    order: 1;
  }
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
This lightbox never moves the video/photo element into itself - it only toggles a class on the SAME
element already sitting in its slide. Moving a live, playing <video> across parents kept surfacing new
problems: the browser re-buffering it, video-media's own [loaded]/[playing] attributes going stale, and
its built-in one-time "click to play" listener firing a second time and colliding with the move.

#{{ lightbox_id }} itself is only used to hide/show the backdrop and the close/prev/next buttons
together via one [hidden] toggle - it carries no styling of its own beyond the theme's own .contents
utility (class="contents"), which is exactly what keeps it a zero-height grid item: every current
child (backdrop, close, prev, next) is position: fixed and so never becomes a real grid item once
hoisted. display: contents also means it can never gain a stacking context of its own the way
position: fixed would - if it did, the close/prev/next buttons' z-index would only be compared
against their siblings INSIDE that context, never able to outrank .is-lightbox-expanded media, which
lives outside it entirely.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__backdrop {
  background: rgb(var(--background));
  z-index: 998;
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
}

{%- comment -%}
.content-box > * (theme.css) sets isolation: isolate on .content-box__background-media, which traps
any z-index/position: fixed inside it to compete only within that local stacking context - it would
never be able to render above anything outside .content-box no matter how high its own z-index is.
Cancelling it here, only while something inside is expanded, lets the fixed media escape correctly.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .content-box__background-media:has(.is-lightbox-expanded) {
  isolation: auto;
}

{%- comment -%}
A `position: fixed` element is normally fixed to the viewport - UNLESS some ancestor between it and
the page root has a transform/perspective/filter/will-change/contain value other than none, in which
case it becomes fixed to THAT ancestor's box instead (even an inert `transform: translateY(0px)`
counts). Some callers wrap their media in an ancestor like that - eg. FAQ's category tab panels get a
`transform: translateY(0px)` entrance-animation style from their tab-switch JS, which otherwise traps
the expanded video/image inside that panel's own (scrolled, not full-height) box instead of the
screen. `*:has()` reaches every ancestor of the one currently expanded item (there is only ever one
at a time, since is-lightbox-expanded is only added to the active item) and strips any transform it
might carry only while that item is open - harmless for ancestors that had none to begin with, and
safe even for ones that did since by the time a visitor manually opens a zoomed item any entrance
animation on it has long since settled.
{%- endcomment -%}
*:has(.is-lightbox-expanded) {
  transform: none !important;
}

{%- comment -%}
Drawer-style framing: the expanded media is centered with padding on every side (same tokens
.drawer::part(body) uses) instead of filling the screen edge to edge, sized to fit within that padded
area without cropping, and rounded to match the item's own corners.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded,
#{{ wrapper_id }} img.is-lightbox-expanded {
  position: fixed;
  z-index: 999;
  inset-block-start: 50%;
  inset-inline-start: 50%;
  translate: -50% -50%;
  width: auto;
  height: auto;
  max-width: 96vw;
  max-height: 96vh;
  border-radius: {{ resolved_corner_radius }};
}

{%- comment -%}
--lightbox-media-w/h drive both the mobile media box AND the close/prev/next positions below, so
those stay pinned to its actual corners instead of the screen's. Height falls back to vh, then
upgrades to 100dvh where supported (same pattern as .modal--age-verifier in theme.css) so it's based
on the real visible area, not the layout viewport Chrome/Safari report before their address bar has
collapsed.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} {
    --lightbox-media-w: min(calc(100vw - 2 * var(--container-gutter)), 32rem);
    --lightbox-media-h: calc(90vh - 2 * var(--container-gutter));
  }
}

@supports (height: 100dvh) {
  @media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
    #{{ wrapper_id }} {
      --lightbox-media-h: calc(90dvh - 2 * var(--container-gutter));
    }
  }
}

@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded {
    width: var(--lightbox-media-w);
    height: var(--lightbox-media-h);
  }

  #{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded video {
    border-radius: inherit;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  #{{ wrapper_id }} img.is-lightbox-expanded {
    max-width: var(--lightbox-media-w);
    max-height: var(--lightbox-media-h);
  }
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded video {
  border-radius: inherit;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} img.is-lightbox-expanded {
  object-fit: contain;
}

#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__close {
  background: var(--close-control-background-color, rgb(var(--button-background-primary)));
  border: 1px solid var(--close-control-border-color, rgb(var(--border-color)));
  color: var(--close-control-icon-color, rgb(var(--button-text-primary)));
  width: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  height: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  border-radius: 100%;
  z-index: 1000;
  place-content: center;
  display: grid;
  position: fixed;
  inset-block-start: 1rem;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
}

{%- comment -%}
Overrides circle-button--xl's own --button-size (2.5rem/3rem) so close/prev/next all match the same
--control-button-size (Theme settings > Global > Controls) the play/pause/sound buttons already use.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev,
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
  width: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  height: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  z-index: 1000;
  position: fixed;
  inset-block-start: 50%;
  translate: 0 -50%;
}

#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev {
  inset-inline-start: 1rem;
}

#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
}

{%- comment -%}
Mobile only: pins close/prev/next to the media box's own corners (using the same --lightbox-media-w/h
it's sized with) instead of the screen's. Not applied on desktop, where the media isn't a fixed-size
box so there's no fixed edge of its own to pin to.

Uses the button's full own width/height (100%), not half (50%), to offset - that lands the button's
edge flush with the anchor point instead of centering the button ON it, so the 0.75rem below reads as
an inward margin that keeps the whole button inside the media box's rounded corners.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__close {
    inset-inline-end: auto;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) calc(var(--lightbox-media-h) / -2 + 0.75rem);
  }

  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev,
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    inset-inline-end: auto;
  }

  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev {
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / -2 + 0.75rem) -50%;
  }

  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) -50%;
  }
}

{%- comment -%}
product-zoom-button (theme.css) inherits currentColor and has a negative inset-inline-end meant to
slightly overhang a card's edge - here it sits inside .content-box's own overflow: hidden, so that
negative offset clips it off-center instead. Pinning both fixes it without touching the shared rule
product-gallery still uses as-is.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .product-zoom-button {
  color: #fff;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
}

{%- comment -%}
product-zoom-button__label (theme.css) starts at width: 0, only revealed on @media (pointer:
fine):hover - on touch devices that hover never fires, so it stays hidden forever. Showing it always
on mobile instead of on hover here.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (pointer: coarse) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .product-zoom-button__label {
    width: auto;
  }
}

{%- comment -%}
The video's own sound/pause toggles stay exactly where they are in the DOM (same as the video itself)
- this just switches them from "absolute, relative to their own .content-box" to "fixed, relative to
the viewport" so they land near the bottom-right of the expanded video instead of the small thumbnail.
display: flex overrides the touch + progress_bar layout's display: none (that rule expects this group
to only ever be visible once relocated into the controls slot, which doesn't apply here).
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls.is-lightbox-expanded {
  opacity: 1 !important;
  pointer-events: auto !important;
  display: flex !important;
  position: fixed !important;
  z-index: 999;
  inset-block-end: var(--drawer-body-padding-block, min(2rem, var(--container-gutter)));
  inset-inline-end: var(--drawer-body-padding-inline, min(2rem, var(--container-gutter)));
  inset-block-start: auto;
}

{%- comment -%}
Mobile only: same corner-pinning as close/prev/next above, so this lands inside the video's own
bottom-right corner instead of the screen's.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls.is-lightbox-expanded {
    inset-block-end: auto;
    inset-inline-end: auto;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) calc(var(--lightbox-media-h) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem);
  }
}
